wasivst 64-bit-in-browser emulator survey (witnessed from project READMEs): (1) v86 = DISQUALIFIED, no 64-bit kernel. (2) Box64 (ptitSeb) = x86-64 userspace emu with Wine64/WOW64 support BUT DynaRec backends are only Arm/RISC-V/LoongArch and it links NATIVE host libs (libc/SDL/GL) - no wasm backend, not browser-viable without a new wasm dynarec. (3) blink (jart) = x86-64 userspace interpreter+JIT, tiny (221KB), C11, JIT ONLY in long mode, but runs x86-64-LINUX binaries and cannot boot Windows; would need Linux Wine on top to load Win64 PE. blinks JIT copies compiled C micro-ops at runtime (native codegen) which does NOT translate to wasm - in wasm it runs interpreter-only (~10x slower per its -j note). (4) QEMU TCG full-system supports x86-64 long mode (unlike v86) and TCG-to-wasm forks exist but are large/slow. CONCLUSION: there is no off-the-shelf fast 64-bit-x86 browser engine; every path pays heavy interpretation overhead in wasm. The realtime-budget for live audio is the binding constraint.